In article <1994Aug17.163256.113@exclbr.spcc.com>, mark_zander@mercury.spcc.com (Mark Zander) writes:
|>I've got a Vaxstation 4000 Model 90 running our manufacturing package
|>batch reports.  The machine is averaging 34 I/O's per second during the
|>day.  The data is on HSC disks served thru a VAX 6510 which runs our
|>financials and manufacturing interactive tasks.  The systems spends
|>most of it's time waiting for I/O's to complete.  I need to improve the
|>performance of the I/Os.  What I am planning is connecting the VS4090
|>to the V6510 with FDDI (DEMFA on the V6510, DEFTA-TurboChnl on the
|>VS4090).  I need to keep the ethernet connections alive on both (we
|>have other vaxstations used as various management stations), but have
|>all the I/O going over the FDDI.
|>
|>1) Does this sound reasonable?

   It is a supported configuration, but there is not enough
   information here to say if it is a reasonable one.

|>2) How do I set the networks up to use bothy controllers (FDDI/Enet)
|>   but just do the I/O from VS4090-V6510 over FDDI?

   OpenVMS automatically supports multiple VMScluster system
   communications services (SCS) paths.  I would not attempt
   to force the system to a particular path.
   
|>3) Anything else I should be concerned with?

   I'd be concerned with why the disks and/or the disk servers
   are running so slowly.  Rather than assuming it's the network
   path, I'd take a step back and figure out where the bottleneck
   is -- upgrading the connection to FDDI won't help things if
   the VAX 6000-510 is overloaded/underconfigured and just can't
   sling the blocks fast enough.  It's also possible that there
   is a problem with the local Ethernet segment...

   The _Guide to OpenVMS Performance Management_ is a good
   starting point for determining the source(s) of most of 
   the common performance problems.  (Apologies if you've
   already read this document.)
-- 

