My standard joke along these lines is that the boxes aren't angular enough to
make decent boat anchors.  

If you're a hardware hacker, you can probably find some decent salvage in a 780
cabinet.  Just one of the power supply modules will be all the bench supply the
typical experimenter will ever need, and if the 780 had a memory backup
battery, that ought to be worth something.  And the Unibus expansion cabinet
makes a perfectly fine 19" rackmount cab, with good service access. 

But as a computer???  Forget it, unless you're running a museum.  Make no
mistake, the 780 *is* a significant part of computing history, as the machine
that brought 32-bit computing and virtual memory to "the masses".   (It was
far, far, FAR from the first machine, or even the first mini or supermini, to
provide either...). 

