Well, there's only one thing to say about this stuff:

	PRESERVE IT !!!

Now, I personally do not have a VAX (nah, too new) but I still feel this is
something we all owe to future generations. There are still prewar cars in
roadworthy condition - and we throw away a valuable part of our profession's
history ?

My basement is full (and hauling a VAX across the puddle would cost a small
fortune) but I have done my part in storing a full PDP 11/05 system (I know
this is small, but it's UNIBUS and it had a full complement of documentation,
spare parts and literature coming with it, since the /05 was the OEM version 
of the /10. It still works without a hitch - no misfeeds on the paper tape,
no broken LEDs, etc. etc. etc.
The most recent DEC I have is a VT78 (That's a "PDP8_IN_A_BOX"). Also works
just fine.
There are other "historical" computers but this being a DEC group would
go too far. Suffice it to say that I can't operate all of 'em simultaneously.
Makes you wonder what happened between that FORTRAN IV compiler in 8K
and your shiny new C++ Dev Sys.. The growth curves of power and memory
requirements have taken entirely different roads..sigh.


Stephen.


